# K62 fix regression pins -- a bare `\E` (not just the four-byte `\Q\E`)
# dissolving right after a range dash, inside a character class.
#
# Not part of the D27-blinded d27/ corpus: these are implementation-authored
# regression pins for docs/dev/known_issues.md K62 (cls_peek_past_dash used
# to recognise only `\Q\E`, so the dash-vs-literal lookahead on `[0-\E]` saw
# a live backslash, committed to a range, and the class's own closing `]`
# was consumed as the high endpoint -- "missing terminating ]" on a legal
# two-member class). Oracle-verified against local libpcre2 10.48-Homebrew
# (this box's libpcre2, per docs/dev/lanes/BOILERPLATE.md's box facts --
# NOT the 10.46 reference oracle; K62's own repro was confirmed the same
# way). Fixed by src/parse/parse.c's cls_dissolve_len, the one dissolution
# rule cls_skip and cls_peek_past_dash now both read.

# a bare \E right after the dash dissolves to nothing, so the dash has no
# high endpoint and is itself a literal member -- {0, -}, matching PCRE2
pattern [0-\E]
features quoting
m "0" 0 1
m "-" 0 1
n "1"
n "9"

# same shape, letter low endpoint -- {a, -}
pattern [a-\E]
features quoting
m "a" 0 1
m "-" 0 1
n "b"

# a bare \E in the MIDDLE of a range (between the dash and a real high
# endpoint) dissolves too, so this is still the ordinary range 0-9, not
# three literal members
pattern [0-\E9]
features quoting
m "0" 0 1
m "5" 0 1
m "9" 0 1
n "-"
n "a"

# \Q\E (the four-byte empty quote K62 says already worked) right after the
# dash is the SAME shape as the first pattern above, oracle-verified here
# too as the fix's own control -- both spellings must answer identically
pattern [0-\Q\E]
features quoting
m "0" 0 1
m "-" 0 1
n "1"

# negative control: a bare \E with the class never closed still refuses --
# the fix must not turn a genuinely unterminated class into a compile
pattern [0-\E
features quoting
# PCRE2 rejects this pattern too: error 106 (missing terminating ] for
# character class) -- provenance only (D26), not a wording pin
perr
